<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Nong Sarai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1593</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Nong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> was fought in early 1593 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suphanburi">Suphan Buri</a>, where King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naresuan">Naresuan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> defeated a Burmese invasion army. According to the chronicles Naresuan killed the Burmese crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mingyi-swa">Mingyi Swa</a> in single combat on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. The victory secured Siamese independence from Toungoo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and is commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> as a national triumph.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-nong-sarai.jpg" alt="Battle of Nong Sarai" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thai Wicharn (ไทวิจารณ์) (probably) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Naresuan_vs_Mingyi_Swa_Elephant_Duel_1592_Painting.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
